<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cupisnique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Cupisnique culture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cupisnique was a culture of the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, flourishing from about 1500 to 500 BCE in valleys such as Jequetepeque, Chicama and Moche. Its people built platform mounds and temples decorated with painted clay friezes of feline and serpent deities, and they made fine stirrup-spout pottery. Closely related to the highland Chavín culture, it formed part of the background from which the later Moche culture developed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cupisnique.jpg" alt="Cupisnique" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Lyndsayruell at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CupisniqueLMC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
